WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.180
- You ready to get crunk, Nahre?

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:03.109
- Is this crunk?

00:00:03.109 --> 00:00:03.942
- This is getting crunk.

00:00:03.942 --> 00:00:04.985
You gotta do like this,
you gotta pull down.

00:00:04.985 --> 00:00:05.818
- Like this?

00:00:05.818 --> 00:00:06.651
Down or up?

00:00:06.651 --> 00:00:08.233
- Pull, pull, pull, pull.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:10.920
- Booming 808 beats,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.330
catchy group vocal hooks,

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:13.480
quick hi-hat rolls,

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.010
and a triplet rap flow.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.730
These are some of the defining
features of trap music,

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.074
today's most popular hip-hop genre.

00:00:20.074 --> 00:00:22.491
(trap music)

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.210
But let's go back 25 years to a group that

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.300
pioneered the sound before
the word trap was even used

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.223
to describe music.

00:00:37.223 --> 00:00:39.890
(hip-hop music)

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.740
- In our episode on trap music,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.810
we covered how the genre
was formed in Atlanta

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.750
by the artists Gucci Mane,
T.I., and Young Jeezy

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.800
to become the phenomenon that it is today.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.570
But now, we want to focus on
a group that came before them

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.923
and laid the groundwork
for trap, Three 6 Mafia.

00:00:55.923 --> 00:00:58.506
(upbeat music)

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.920
What we call trap music
today is really a blend

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.480
of several hip-hop genres
from all over the south.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.730
One of the most influential is crunk,

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:08.960
a slower, more severe style of rap music

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.100
that weave club and party
culture with gang life.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.900
And although Three 6 Mafia are
responsible for popularizing

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.760
the genre, the sound can be traced to

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.900
another Memphis artist, DJ Spanish Fly.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.820
(rap music)

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.559
- I'm not familiar with
any of these artists.

00:01:25.559 --> 00:01:26.490
- Okay, okay.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.170
- Yeah, it's been eye-opening.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.248
- Not even like the newer
stuff like Solja Boy,

00:01:30.248 --> 00:01:31.081
you know about Solja Boy?

00:01:31.081 --> 00:01:32.630
- Yeah, no.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:34.522
No, not at all.

00:01:34.522 --> 00:01:36.870
(laughing)

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.670
For me as a musician,
hearing the different

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:42.350
elements of what goes in
to music, like crunk music,

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.100
it makes me kind of think about

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.000
oh, these specific musical
elements really allow

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.460
the musician to tap into
these types of emotions,

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.010
and I think that's fascinating.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.960
Sonically, crunk emphasizes
aggression and tension

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.400
via minor chords, slower
tempos, distorted bass,

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.040
contrasting rhythms, and
often abbrasive tambers.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.170
You can hear this in the
video game-like beats

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:07.843
of Tommy Wright III.

00:02:07.843 --> 00:02:10.176
(rap music)

00:02:11.233 --> 00:02:13.450
Early crunk producers
often use these homemade

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.330
sounds in place of the standard hip-hop

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.860
building blocks of funk and soul samples

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.820
used by east coast rappers.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:22.080
This sound was paralleled
by the growing popularity

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.730
of a dancing style called gangsta walking,

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.260
which moved between
elegant, fluid footwork

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.010
to dramatic pop-locking, and stomps.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.593
(upbeat music)

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.799
- In the early 90's,
Three 6 Mafia emerged from

00:02:40.799 --> 00:02:43.150
Memphis's DIY mix tape community

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.250
where local artists would create and sell

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.270
cassette tapes that mixed their own music

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:48.640
with popular hip-hop.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:49.818
While in high school,

00:02:49.818 --> 00:02:50.670
(bell ringing)

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.320
DJ Paul and Lord Infamous
formed The Serial Killaz

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:54.800
and put out regular mix tapes,

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.440
adding their own production in layers

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.010
of triplet rap flows to hip-hop hits.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.460
These self produced
hybrid playlists allowed

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.320
up and coming and entrepreneurial rappers

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:06.390
to promote their music and
make money in the process.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.430
In the early 90's, the duo began working

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:09.652
with other local artists.

00:03:09.652 --> 00:03:11.992
After adding producer, Juicy J, rappers

00:03:11.992 --> 00:03:15.490
Gangsta Boo, Crunchy
Black, and Koopsta joined,

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:18.070
and the group took on
the name Three 6 Mafia.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:19.880
As crunk became more popular in Memphis

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.350
during the early 90's,
the city's black community

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.380
was under siege from the crack epidemic.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:25.980
Increasing street violence made it one of

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.660
America's most dangerous cities.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.750
The hard hitting sound of Three 6 Mafia

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.880
echoed the rawness of these issues.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.901
The vocal chance, Lord
Infamous's triplet flows,

00:03:34.901 --> 00:03:37.220
and DJ Paul's menacing minimalist beats

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.210
created the model for early crunk

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:41.093
and the sonic template for trap music.

00:03:41.093 --> 00:03:44.260
(Three 6 Mafia plays)

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:46.710
- Like it's ultra-violent lyrics,

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.090
Three 6 Mafia emphasized tension sonically

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:49.960
in the music.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.550
In their 1997 track Prophet Posse,

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:54.380
the sample of the trick a man beat is in

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.100
a different key than the
synthesizer melodies,

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:59.200
creating a poly-tonal harmony,

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.290
the simultaneous use of two or more keys

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:02.657
in a musical composition.

00:04:02.657 --> 00:04:05.240
(upbeat music)

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.470
Of course it's not a new trick,

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:10.600
this technique has been
used by many composers,

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.250
including myself, to
create tension between

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:16.250
what most listeners expect,
a standard progression

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.450
that resolves to a stable
tonic and what we get,

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:20.295
distance.

00:04:20.295 --> 00:04:23.010
So, this is an example of poly-harmony.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.910
We have one key, E major.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:28.180
Another one, F major.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:29.163
Combine them.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.514
Something like this.

00:04:32.514 --> 00:04:35.014
(piano plays)

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:43.170
Three 6 Mafia's beats
laid down the groundwork

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:44.830
for today's trap music.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.590
Their songs amp up a listener through

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:49.470
heavy bass lines, synthesizer melodies,

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.620
minor key harmonies that
create a foreboding mood,

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.080
and of course the role in 808.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.240
We can even hear how these
intermittent hi-hat rolls

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.130
would turn into the
rapid fire hi-hat sound

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:02.379
of Atlanta Trap.

00:05:02.379 --> 00:05:04.962
(upbeat music)

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.100
- These are not the only
way Three 6 has inspired

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.195
today's rap music.

00:05:09.195 --> 00:05:11.806
Today, we call the
double time triplet flow

00:05:11.806 --> 00:05:13.414
the Migos flow.

00:05:13.414 --> 00:05:16.081
(Migos playing)

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.603
But it's actually Lord
Infamous of Three 6 Mafia

00:05:19.603 --> 00:05:21.701
who's credited as the originator.

00:05:21.701 --> 00:05:24.034
(rap music)

00:05:25.986 --> 00:05:28.230
While this claim caused
beef between Three 6 Mafia

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.180
and Cleveland's Bone Thugs N Harmony,

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.480
the Memphis flow was declamatory,

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.760
in your face, regular in meter,

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:36.310
and more staccato than Bone Thugs's

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:39.250
harmonic and more rhythmically
complex group rapping.

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.009
Here's the trap superstar, 2 Chainz,

00:05:41.009 --> 00:05:43.556
stating this claim in a song, Trap Back.

00:05:43.556 --> 00:05:46.556
("Trap Back" plays)

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.220
In the early 2000's,
crunk rocked the charts.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.550
Songs by Atlanta based
artists like Lil Jon

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:54.660
and The East Side Boyz's Get Low,

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.390
and the Ying Yang Twins' Salt Shaker,

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.860
had cross over popularity
both in dance clubs

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.350
and in the strip club scene.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:03.380
Variations of crunk began
emerging on hip-hop radio

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:04.587
like snap music.

00:06:04.587 --> 00:06:07.620
("Crank that (Solja Boy)" plays)

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:08.880
Somewhere during this time,

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.560
the sounds of crunk, snap, trap,

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:13.400
and other hip-hop genres from the south

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:14.503
all merged into one.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:17.340
Today, we just call it all trap,

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.960
but it's really a mix
of New Orleans bounce,

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:21.400
Houston chopped and screwed,

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.170
Miami bass, and Memphis crunk.

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:26.830
- It's hard to point the
origin to one city or artist,

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:29.370
but Three 6 Mafia were
instrumental in creating

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.940
the sound of modern hip-hop.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:32.960
Their inspiration can
be heard in everything

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:36.240
from the Migos flow to A$AP Mob's beats.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.490
Some artists even go so far
as copying the group chants.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:41.740
Rapper, Travis Scott, was sued by DJ Paul

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:43.640
for using the tear the club up chant

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:46.680
in this 2018 song, No Bystanders.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:50.013
("No Bystanders" plays)

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:52.900
- Nahre and I are gonna
create a Three 6 Mafia

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:53.850
inspired track.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.300
The simplicity is they made,

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.100
notice all this stuff
going on in the track.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:57.933
- Mhm.

00:06:57.933 --> 00:06:59.550
- And it still sounds simple.

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:01.790
Maybe it's because of the poly-tonalilty.

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:02.623
- It has to rub.

00:07:02.623 --> 00:07:04.963
The different chords have to rub.

00:07:04.963 --> 00:07:06.092
Let's see.

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:09.009
